    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we start by taking the square root of both sides. This step is crucial because it eliminates the square on the left side, leaving us with two possible equations since both positive and negative squares of a real number yield the same result. Thus,\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3.\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve each of these equations for \\(x\\):\n\n1. For the first equation \\(x - 5 = 3\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 3 + 5\n\\]\n\nSo, \n\n\\[\nx = 8.\n\\]\n\n2. For the second equation \\(x - 5 = -3\\):\n\n\\[\nx = -3 + 5\n\\]\n\nSo, \n\n\\[\nx = 2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\). \n\nThe sum of these roots is:\n\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots of the given equation is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
